istorical record. Relevant to cause-and-effect analysis.", "newValue": "Brandeis University, established on October 20, 1948, in Waltham, Massachusetts, stands as a pivotal institution in the landscape of American higher education. Founded in the aftermath of World War II, it was named after Louis Brandeis, the first Jewish Justice of the U.S. Supreme Court, reflecting the university's commitment to social justice and intellectual rigor. As a private research university, it has cultivated a diverse academic environment, emphasizing liberal arts, sciences, and social justice, thus influencing educational practices in the United States and beyond.

